WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:12.040
Since failure is not an option, we need to
figure out what to do next, and we will try

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.380
to do it by learning a little bit of biology.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:21.380
And biology will give us a hint on how we
should implement our algorithms.

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:27.689
Some of you may find the description of somewhat
convoluted biological concepts that I am about

00:00:27.689 --> 00:00:31.460
to present difficult to digest.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:38.460
If you feel like this, don't worry; if you
believe me that DNA replication is an asymmetric

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:45.760
process, then you can simply skip this part
and go to the point where we derive from this

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:49.440
biological knowledge how to design efficient
algorithms.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:56.440
The first thing to remind ourselves is that
DNA strands have directions, and the two strands

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.510
of DNA run in opposite directions.

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:07.510
Here on this slide, the blue strand runs clockwise,
and the green strand runs counterclockwise.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:15.390
If you were a DNA polymerase, how would you
replicate a genome?

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:20.870
If I was a DNA polymerase, I would do something
very simple.

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:27.870
I would wait until DNA unwinds a little bit,
recruit 4 DNA polymerases, and I would just

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:33.090
move them along the genome, trying to replicate.

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:40.090
It looks like just 4 DNA polymerases are enough
to replicate the whole genome.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:47.120
And when the replication fork enlarges, I
continue and continue the replication process.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:52.000
This is simple but completely wrong.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:57.560
And if there are biology professors attending
this lecture, they are probably already writing

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:03.170
a petition to fire me and send me to a "Biology
101" camp.

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:09.789
The reason why it does not work is that DNA
polymerases are unidirectional.

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:16.789
They can only copy DNA in the direction that
is opposite to the direction of DNA, which

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:25.380
means that when we want to recruit four DNA
polymerases, two of them, this one and this

00:02:27.709 --> 00:02:33.230
one, will be working just fine, but the two
others won't be able to move because they

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:37.750
cannot move in the same direction as the direction
of DNA.

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:44.750
Then we can classify DNA strands into four
half-strands.

00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:52.110
The strand that I showed you (the blue strand
that goes from origin to terminus) is the

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:57.760
reverse half-strand, and I have no problem
replicating it because moving from the origin

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:02.700
to terminus goes in the opposite direction
to DNA.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:09.700
Likewise, this thick green line that I show
right now also does not present any problem

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:15.020
replicating -- one DNA polymerase can accomplish
it.

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:22.020
But the two other half-strands present a big,
big problem because we cannot move in the

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:25.750
same direction that they go.

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:31.870
So, if you were a unidirectional DNA polymerase,
how would you replicate a genome?

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:35.930
Here is a potential solution.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:42.930
Wait until the fork enlarges, and when it
enlarges, start replicating it in the same

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:45.459
direction to DNA,

00:03:45.459 --> 00:03:51.620
When the fork enlarges a little bit more,
you put another DNA polymerase and continue

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:52.290
replicating.

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:58.060
Four DNA polymerases wont be enough because
each of these DNA polymerases that I recruited

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:03.700
copied just approximately 3000 nucleotides,
so that we need a huge number of DNA polymerases

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:05.800
to proceed this way.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:12.550
You can see that the resulting fragments,
many fragments that are being built (called

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:16.120
Okazaki fragments) complicate our life a little
bit.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:21.979
But, in the end, after this process is over,
we will have many Okazaki fragments, so that

00:04:21.979 --> 00:04:27.110
we will have the genome copied in many, many
little fragments.

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:32.960
And the only thing we need afterwards is to
repair the gaps between different Okazaki

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:35.749
fragments and to replicate the genome.

00:04:35.749 --> 00:04:42.749
So the only thing we need to learn about this
process to proceed with our algorithms afterwards

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:50.580
is that the reverse and forward half-strand
(the thick half-strands and thin half-strands)

00:04:51.349 --> 00:04:53.589
have very different lifestyles.

00:04:53.589 --> 00:04:59.839
The reverse half-strand lives a double-stranded
life because it is constantly replicated;

00:04:59.839 --> 00:05:04.059
there is hardly a moment when it lives double
stranded [sic -- should be "single stranded"].

00:05:04.059 --> 00:05:10.279
But the forward half-strands spend a large
portion of their lives single-stranded because

00:05:10.279 --> 00:05:15.680
it has to wait until the replication fork
opens and until it starts replication.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:22.680
I hope this is clear, but there is one looming
question: "Why would a computer scientist

00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:28.270
care?"

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:34.689
And we will learn in the next segment why
a computer scientist should care about this.

00:05:34.689 --> 00:05:41.689
So, asymmetry of replication affects nucleotide
frequencies. Why? Let's think about this.

00:05:44.059 --> 00:05:50.479
Single-stranded DNA has a much higher mutation
rate than double-stranded DNA.

00:05:50.479 --> 00:05:57.479
That's why, if one nucleotide has a greater
mutation rate, then we should observe a shortage

00:05:57.919 --> 00:06:04.919
of this nucleotide on the forward half-strand
because it lives a single-stranded life.

00:06:06.159 --> 00:06:13.159
Which nucleotide, A, C, G, or T has a higher
mutation rate? And why?

00:06:14.009 --> 00:06:21.009
It turns out that there is a peculiar statistic
of mutation rates of nucleotides, and cytosine

00:06:22.479 --> 00:06:26.919
(C) rapidly mutates into T.

00:06:26.919 --> 00:06:33.919
What is quite amazing is that through this
deamination process, rate of mutations rise

00:06:34.849 --> 00:06:41.849
100-fold when DNA is single-stranded, which
means that the strand that lives the single-stranded

00:06:42.669 --> 00:06:48.099
life very quickly gets depleted from C.

00:06:48.099 --> 00:06:51.539
What does it mean for us as algorithm designers?

00:06:51.539 --> 00:06:57.809
Forward half-strands that live the single-stranded
life have a shortage of C and normal G.

00:06:57.809 --> 00:07:03.819
Reverse half-strands that live the double-stranded
life have a shortage of G and normal C.

00:07:03.819 --> 00:07:09.149
Now, keeping this in mind, let's take a walk
long the genome.

00:07:09.149 --> 00:07:15.509
We start at the terminus and, let's say we
move, according to the red line, from the

00:07:15.509 --> 00:07:17.379
terminus to the origin.

00:07:17.379 --> 00:07:24.379
In this case, we move along the strand in
which C is high and G is low, which means

00:07:25.249 --> 00:07:32.110
that #G -- #C is decreasing as we walk.

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:39.110
But when we walk along this half-strand, C
is low and G is high, which means that #G

00:07:40.639 --> 00:07:46.899
-- #C, total number of nucleotides G minus
the total number of nucleotides C, is decreasing

00:07:46.899 --> 00:07:48.789
as we walk.

00:07:48.789 --> 00:07:54.759
Again, this sounds like a peculiar and not
very important thing, why do we care?

00:07:54.759 --> 00:07:57.059
I ask one more question:

00:07:57.059 --> 00:08:04.059
If you walk along a genome and you count the
number of G minus the number of C that you

00:08:05.469 --> 00:08:12.469
saw, and you have been seeing that #G -- #C
has been decreasing, and suddenly starts increasing...

00:08:14.919 --> 00:08:21.389
Imagine, just imagine, you walk through the
genome, you count the difference #G -- #C,

00:08:21.389 --> 00:08:24.550
and it has been decreasing, and suddenly starts
increasing.

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:29.469
My question is: "Where are in the genome are
you?"

00:08:29.469 --> 00:08:36.469
And to figure out where in the genome you
are, we need once again to look at peculiar

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:39.070
statistics.

00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:46.070
The only place in the genome where the behavior
of #G -- #C switches from decreasing to increasing

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:49.410
is the origin of replication.

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:54.700
Which means that if you walk along the genome
and see that #G -- #C has been decreasing

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:59.920
and suddenly starts increasing, it means you
just passed the origin of replication.

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:03.120
And this is the hint for our algorithm.

